00:40:54.640 --> 00:40:58.320
And Phil observed that in decision-making and judgment,

1005
00:40:58.320 --> 00:40:59.640
we spend a lot of time thinking

1006
00:40:59.640 --> 00:41:02.470
like preachers, prosecutors, and politicians.

1007
00:41:02.470 --> 00:41:04.900
And we're usually not aware of it when we're doing it.

1008
00:41:04.900 --> 00:41:08.110
A preacher mindset is essentially believing

1009
00:41:08.110 --> 00:41:10.060
that you've already seen the light

1010
00:41:10.060 --> 00:41:13.050
and now you have to go and proselytize to everybody else

1011
00:41:13.050 --> 00:41:14.460
and get them onboard.

1012
00:41:14.460 --> 00:41:16.880
So you've drunk the intellectual Kool-Aid

1013
00:41:16.880 --> 00:41:18.563
and now your job is to serve it.

1014
00:41:19.470 --> 00:41:21.060
A prosecuting mindset

1015
00:41:21.060 --> 00:41:23.400
is more about proving everyone else wrong

1016
00:41:23.400 --> 00:41:25.970
and winning your side of the case.

1017
00:41:25.970 --> 00:41:28.300
And then a politician mindset

1018
00:41:28.300 --> 00:41:30.010
is really about saying,

1019
00:41:30.010 --> 00:41:32.000
okay, I've got some constituents here

1020
00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:34.200
and I need to lobby for their buy-in.

1021
00:41:34.200 --> 00:41:35.900
I want their support.

1022
00:41:35.900 --> 00:41:38.590
And my big worry, Rich, was when I learned

1023
00:41:38.590 --> 00:41:39.750
about this framework, I thought,

1024
00:41:39.750 --> 00:41:42.844
okay, preaching and prosecuting,

1025
00:41:42.844 --> 00:41:45.110
those stop you from thinking again.

1026
00:41:45.110 --> 00:41:48.050
Because if I know I'm right and I'm sure you're wrong,

