1305
00:54:32.530 --> 00:54:33.363
It takes what it takes, man.

1306
00:54:33.363 --> 00:54:35.670
No, that story is where you talk

1307
00:54:35.670 --> 00:54:39.040
about this idea of wiggle room.

1308
00:54:39.040 --> 00:54:43.160
Like, even if you feel like you're on a good path

1309
00:54:43.160 --> 00:54:46.120
for yourself, like not holding on too tightly to it

1310
00:54:46.120 --> 00:54:49.550
and always making room to be malleable-

1311
00:54:49.550 --> 00:54:50.630
To be inspired.

1312
00:54:50.630 --> 00:54:52.180
So that you can be available

1313
00:54:52.180 --> 00:54:56.630
for the magical opportunity of going to India

1314
00:54:56.630 --> 00:54:57.527
in the case of your story.

1315
00:54:57.527 --> 00:55:00.960
And it's not about, 'cause some people may hear that

1316
00:55:00.960 --> 00:55:03.190
and go, "That's just you not committing

1317
00:55:03.190 --> 00:55:05.700
to whatever you said you were gonna do."

1318
00:55:05.700 --> 00:55:07.937
And I would refine that and say,

1319
00:55:07.937 --> 00:55:11.500
"Well, it's really your feeling

1320
00:55:11.500 --> 00:55:14.180
about your path, whatever that is,

1321
00:55:14.180 --> 00:55:15.440
whether you have language for it or not

1322
00:55:15.440 --> 00:55:17.740
is always gonna be there

1323
00:55:17.740 --> 00:55:22.220
but the day-to-day plans may change

1324
00:55:22.220 --> 00:55:25.310
about how you get there or what occupation

1325
00:55:25.310 --> 00:55:27.780
or what family lifestyle choices

1326
00:55:27.780 --> 00:55:29.087
you have to go through in order to get there.

1327
00:55:29.087 --> 00:55:31.240
And so that's where you wanna be malleable

1328
00:55:31.240 --> 00:55:33.330
and be open to inspiration.

1329
00:55:33.330 --> 00:55:37.270
Not in questioning the ultimate goal or plan.

1330
00:55:37.270 --> 00:55:39.010
You really can't go wrong

1331
00:55:39.010 --> 00:55:41.510
if you just keep following

1332
00:55:41.510 --> 00:55:43.270
whatever you think inside.
Sure, but you also

1333
00:55:43.270 --> 00:55:47.650
can't expect to be smiled upon by perhaps your peers

1334
00:55:47.650 --> 00:55:48.483
and your colleagues.

1335
00:55:48.483 --> 00:55:49.316
Because that will-
No, it takes you

1336
00:55:49.316 --> 00:55:51.610
completely away from social proof.

1337
00:55:51.610 --> 00:55:53.290
You're gonna look like a dilettante.

1338
00:55:53.290 --> 00:55:54.123
You know what I mean?

1339
00:55:54.123 --> 00:55:56.150
Like, oh, this guy just goes from one thing to the next.

1340
00:55:56.150 --> 00:55:58.950
He can't really stick with anything.

1341
00:55:58.950 --> 00:55:59.783
Did you read

1342
00:56:01.920 --> 00:56:03.560
David Epstein's book "Range"?

1343
00:56:03.560 --> 00:56:06.210
No.
So it's a very interesting

1344
00:56:06.210 --> 00:56:08.100
kind of study

1345
00:56:08.100 --> 00:56:11.920
of a variety of people

1346
00:56:11.920 --> 00:56:13.960
who have excelled in different fields

1347
00:56:13.960 --> 00:56:15.820
from science and education

1348
00:56:15.820 --> 00:56:19.410
to athletics and art, et cetera.

1349
00:56:19.410 --> 00:56:21.230
And it belies

1350
00:56:21.230 --> 00:56:23.917
this sort of Malcolm Gladwell myth

1351
00:56:23.917 --> 00:56:26.360
of the 10,000-Hour Rule

1352
00:56:26.360 --> 00:56:28.640
and demonstrates that there are those people,

1353
00:56:28.640 --> 00:56:30.780
of course, the Tiger Woods and people like that.

1354
00:56:30.780 --> 00:56:32.800
But by and large,

1355
00:56:32.800 --> 00:56:35.570
most people who are fully expressed and excelling

1356
00:56:35.570 --> 00:56:39.710
at the highest level of their specialized field

1357
00:56:39.710 --> 00:56:42.040
are people who earlier in life

1358
00:56:42.040 --> 00:56:44.570
did lots of different stuff and were the people

1359
00:56:44.570 --> 00:56:46.270
who just really couldn't commit to anything.

1360
00:56:46.270 --> 00:56:51.200
And as a result, they develop this robustness of experience

1361
00:56:51.200 --> 00:56:54.090
that back to that idea of in retrospect,

1362
00:56:54.090 --> 00:56:55.660
all the dots line up,

1363
00:56:55.660 --> 00:56:58.330
make perfect sense with where they end up,

1364
00:56:58.330 --> 00:57:01.020
but they're people of varied interests

1365
00:57:01.020 --> 00:57:02.670
and many different passions

1366
00:57:02.670 --> 00:57:05.270
who developed that capacity early

1367
00:57:05.270 --> 00:57:07.410
to be malleable and flexible,

1368
00:57:07.410 --> 00:57:09.660
to have that wiggle room.

1369
00:57:09.660 --> 00:57:12.590
And it's those people that we look at now

1370
00:57:12.590 --> 00:57:14.130
as lazy dilettantes,

1371
00:57:14.130 --> 00:57:17.170
who ultimately it's reframing that

1372
00:57:17.170 --> 00:57:19.070
and saying, well, and you talk about this.

1373
00:57:19.070 --> 00:57:21.530
Like how do you suspend judgment in the book?

1374
00:57:21.530 --> 00:57:23.830
Maybe they're like we should look at this

1375
00:57:23.830 --> 00:57:24.740
a little bit differently.

1376
00:57:24.740 --> 00:57:26.920
Perhaps they're in their exploratory phase

1377
00:57:26.920 --> 00:57:29.340
and they're gonna to be magnificent and amazing

1378
00:57:29.340 --> 00:57:32.100
and world changing in something that they finally latch onto

1379
00:57:32.100 --> 00:57:33.510
later in life.

1380
00:57:33.510 --> 00:57:36.830
Yeah, there is a story in there about how (chuckles),

1381
00:57:36.830 --> 00:57:38.810
I talk about leaving that first job

1382
00:57:38.810 --> 00:57:40.363
for the reasons that I left.

1383
00:57:42.096 --> 00:57:43.310
And then while I was writing,

1384
00:57:43.310 --> 00:57:44.540
I remember the day I wrote that story,

1385
00:57:44.540 --> 00:57:46.963
I was in an Airbnb, I think it was in London,

1386
00:57:47.810 --> 00:57:49.410
living out of a backpack,

1387
00:57:49.410 --> 00:57:52.050
didn't own anything, still don't own anything.

1388
00:57:52.050 --> 00:57:53.240
I've got a little money saved up,

1389
00:57:53.240 --> 00:57:54.920
but I had less saved up at the time.

1390
00:57:54.920 --> 00:57:57.340
And I was like, you know, compared to my peers,

1391
00:57:57.340 --> 00:57:59.250
I don't really, I haven't succeeded.

1392
00:57:59.250 --> 00:58:01.623
I haven't really done anything,

1393
00:58:04.130 --> 00:58:08.580
that led to material, the material success

1394
00:58:08.580 --> 00:58:11.520
that we kind of aspire towards today,

1395
00:58:11.520 --> 00:58:13.810
but I've never felt more fulfilled.

1396
00:58:13.810 --> 00:58:15.430
And I realized that the true wealth

1397
00:58:15.430 --> 00:58:17.760
is the fulfillment inside, right?

1398
00:58:17.760 --> 00:58:21.060
And that's, again, this is a living experiment. (chuckles)

1399
00:58:21.060 --> 00:58:22.220
Yeah.
And it's not

1400
00:58:22.220 --> 00:58:23.410
that I'm like walking around

1401
00:58:23.410 --> 00:58:26.490
beaming all the time because I feel fulfilled inside.

1402
00:58:26.490 --> 00:58:28.310
That's not really what we're talking about.

1403
00:58:28.310 --> 00:58:32.340
It's just, it's having this inner security

1404
00:58:32.340 --> 00:58:33.870
that wherever you are

1405
00:58:33.870 --> 00:58:35.350
is where you're supposed to be.

1406
00:58:35.350 --> 00:58:38.320
And if that means you get challenged in certain ways,

1407
00:58:38.320 --> 00:58:41.370
then that challenge is there for you

1408
00:58:41.370 --> 00:58:43.667
to learn something new about yourself.

1409
00:58:43.667 --> 00:58:46.910
And that new thing that you learn is going to add

1410
00:58:46.910 --> 00:58:49.443
to the feeling tone of fulfillment, right?

1411
00:58:51.140 --> 00:58:53.330
And so that's the underlying message

1412
00:58:53.330 --> 00:58:56.220
of this whole path is your path is not leading you

1413
00:58:56.220 --> 00:58:57.700
to more success or more comfort.

1414
00:58:57.700 --> 00:58:59.650
It's leading you to know yourself better

1415
00:58:59.650 --> 00:59:03.080
and ultimately to feel your connection with other people.

1416
00:59:03.080 --> 00:59:05.920
And that could be a very, very treacherous path

1417
00:59:05.920 --> 00:59:06.930
at some points.

1418
00:59:06.930 --> 00:59:11.223
Could be incredibly uncomfortable at other points.

1419
00:59:12.760 --> 00:59:16.140
But the happiness that people tend to look for outside

1420
00:59:16.140 --> 00:59:20.280
in success is much more temporal

1421
00:59:20.280 --> 00:59:22.330
compared to what can feel inside

1422
00:59:22.330 --> 00:59:25.970
while you're going through all of those other moments

1423
00:59:25.970 --> 00:59:28.300
and obstacles in life.

1424
00:59:28.300 --> 00:59:29.560
All right, people, up next

1425
00:59:29.560 --> 00:59:32.240
is one of the world's leading authorities

1426
00:59:32.240 --> 00:59:36.790
on the neuroscience of addiction, Anna Lembke MD,

1427
00:59:36.790 --> 00:59:39.640
author of two important books, "Drug Dealer MD"

1428
00:59:39.640 --> 00:59:42.660
and her newest work "Dopamine Nation",

1429
00:59:42.660 --> 00:59:46.270
in episode 623, she gave us a powerful primer

1430
00:59:46.270 --> 00:59:49.950
on why the relentless pursuit of pleasure

1431
00:59:49.950 --> 00:59:51.893
always leads to pain.

1432
00:59:54.070 --> 00:59:55.720
For so long we've had this idea

1433
00:59:56.591 --> 01:00:00.360
that addiction is a downstream consequence

1434
01:00:00.360 --> 01:00:01.800
of some other problem.

1435
01:00:01.800 --> 01:00:04.020
But addiction is its own problem.

1436
01:00:04.020 --> 01:00:07.450
You can have a perfect life and get very, very addicted.

1437
01:00:07.450 --> 01:00:10.330
Yeah, yeah, that's a really important point.

1438
01:00:10.330 --> 01:00:14.900
You know I've had conversations with Gabor Mate

1439
01:00:14.900 --> 01:00:17.360
and for him, it's all about childhood trauma.

1440
01:00:17.360 --> 01:00:19.300
Resolve that, you resolve the addiction.

1441
01:00:19.300 --> 01:00:20.870
I had Johann Hari here.

1442
01:00:20.870 --> 01:00:22.930
For him, it's all about lost connections,

1443
01:00:22.930 --> 01:00:25.890
your connectivity to your friends and your family

1444
01:00:25.890 --> 01:00:26.723
and your community.

1445
01:00:26.723 --> 01:00:30.730
And I think those are important pieces in this puzzle,

1446
01:00:30.730 --> 01:00:31.950
but I'm reluctant

1447
01:00:33.501 --> 01:00:35.640
to be reductionist

1448
01:00:35.640 --> 01:00:37.890
about the role that those play

1449
01:00:37.890 --> 01:00:40.950
in the broader context of addiction.

1450
01:00:40.950 --> 01:00:44.160
Thank you so much for invoking those examples

1451
01:00:44.160 --> 01:00:47.800
because that shows me that you perfectly understand

1452
01:00:47.800 --> 01:00:49.940
what I'm trying to communicate here.

1453
01:00:49.940 --> 01:00:52.850
Because, yes, it's true that childhood trauma

1454
01:00:52.850 --> 01:00:55.300
increases your risk for developing addiction.

1455
01:00:55.300 --> 01:00:59.180
Yes, it's true that addiction leads to isolation

1456
01:00:59.180 --> 01:01:01.570
and that the antidote to addiction

1457
01:01:01.570 --> 01:01:03.950
is social connection in part.

1458
01:01:03.950 --> 01:01:07.210
But what is so important to understand

1459
01:01:07.210 --> 01:01:09.530
is you can have the perfect childhood,

1460
01:01:09.530 --> 01:01:11.020
the perfect parents,

1461
01:01:11.020 --> 01:01:12.810
the greatest social network,

1462
01:01:12.810 --> 01:01:15.150
the best spouse, wonderful kids,

1463
01:01:15.150 --> 01:01:18.230
and you can get really, really addicted.

1464
01:01:18.230 --> 01:01:21.490
And that is so important for people to understand

1465
01:01:21.490 --> 01:01:23.440
and also healthcare providers.

1466
01:01:23.440 --> 01:01:24.940
Because everybody's sort of looking

1467
01:01:24.940 --> 01:01:27.130
for the reason behind the addiction,

1468
01:01:27.130 --> 01:01:30.000
but there doesn't have to be a reason behind addiction.

1469
01:01:30.000 --> 01:01:32.517
Addiction just can be on its own.

1470
01:01:32.517 --> 01:01:35.030
Yeah, I think that's a crucial point.

1471
01:01:35.030 --> 01:01:37.570
I mean, I get asked very frequently,

1472
01:01:37.570 --> 01:01:39.570
like, "What do you think caused this?

1473
01:01:39.570 --> 01:01:41.270
What is the source of your addiction?"

1474
01:01:41.270 --> 01:01:45.420
And I'm heavily indoctrinated in 12-step

1475
01:01:45.420 --> 01:01:48.020
and I'm sure I have some biases around that

1476
01:01:48.020 --> 01:01:49.360
but one of the things that you learn

1477
01:01:49.360 --> 01:01:52.260
is that it's fine if you wanna psychoanalyze

1478
01:01:53.830 --> 01:01:56.390
that aspect of your origin story,

1479
01:01:56.390 --> 01:01:58.960
but ultimately it doesn't avail you with the tools

1480
01:01:58.960 --> 01:02:00.020
for how to live today

1481
01:02:00.020 --> 01:02:03.610
and how to move productively forward.

1482
01:02:03.610 --> 01:02:07.500
And there is an argument to be made that it's sort of

1483
01:02:07.500 --> 01:02:10.770
a fool's errand to spend too much time on that.

1484
01:02:10.770 --> 01:02:13.077
Yes. Insight can be the booby prize.

1485
01:02:13.077 --> 01:02:14.190
Yeah.

1486
01:02:14.190 --> 01:02:18.100
Yeah, no, this is a really very, very important concept.

1487
01:02:18.100 --> 01:02:20.090
And the other reason it's important

1488
01:02:20.090 --> 01:02:23.390
is because I think we're natural storytellers

1489
01:02:23.390 --> 01:02:26.710
and we want to rationalize irrational behavior.

1490
01:02:26.710 --> 01:02:30.380
And so the first thing that we try to do

1491
01:02:30.380 --> 01:02:32.600
when we are doing something that's irrational

1492
01:02:32.600 --> 01:02:34.390
and self and other destructive,

1493
01:02:34.390 --> 01:02:36.350
is to tell ourselves a story

1494
01:02:36.350 --> 01:02:38.220
or have someone else tell us a story

1495
01:02:38.220 --> 01:02:41.160
about why we would do that crazy thing.

1496
01:02:41.160 --> 01:02:42.790
And if we can solve that equation,

1497
01:02:42.790 --> 01:02:44.860
then suddenly every everything makes sense

1498
01:02:44.860 --> 01:02:46.648
and you can figure this out.

1499
01:02:46.648 --> 01:02:48.370
It doesn't work that way.
No, no.

1500
01:02:48.370 --> 01:02:51.960
I mean, I have had over 20 plus years,

1501
01:02:51.960 --> 01:02:53.750
countless patients come into my office

1502
01:02:53.750 --> 01:02:55.510
and say, Dr. Lembke,

1503
01:02:55.510 --> 01:02:57.350
the reason that I'm addicted to alcohol

1504
01:02:57.350 --> 01:03:00.700
or I'm addicted to cannabis or I'm a compulsive gambler

1505
01:03:00.700 --> 01:03:02.320
is because I'm depressed and anxious.

1506
01:03:02.320 --> 01:03:03.573
And if you would just fix that,

1507
01:03:03.573 --> 01:03:05.940
then I wouldn't have that other problem.

1508
01:03:05.940 --> 01:03:07.660
And what I have to do is say to them,

1509
01:03:07.660 --> 01:03:10.280
you know what, I wish that were true.

1510
01:03:10.280 --> 01:03:12.670
But here's the truth, number one,

1511
01:03:12.670 --> 01:03:15.040
even if I could magically wave my wand

1512
01:03:15.040 --> 01:03:17.080
and make your depression and anxiety go away,

1513
01:03:17.080 --> 01:03:19.070
once you're addicted, you're addicted.

1514
01:03:19.070 --> 01:03:20.950
And if we don't focus on that problem,

1515
01:03:20.950 --> 01:03:23.250
that's not gonna get better.

1516
01:03:23.250 --> 01:03:26.330
The other thing is that relationship

1517
01:03:26.330 --> 01:03:30.050
between psychological symptoms and addiction,

1518
01:03:30.050 --> 01:03:32.670
it's complicated and it's not necessarily

1519
01:03:32.670 --> 01:03:34.540
that the depression comes first

1520
01:03:34.540 --> 01:03:35.820
and then the addiction comes.

1521
01:03:35.820 --> 01:03:38.040
Addiction can lead to depression, anxiety,

1522
01:03:38.040 --> 01:03:41.160
which is why my first intervention so often

1523
01:03:41.160 --> 01:03:43.030
is to have people abstain.

1524
01:03:43.030 --> 01:03:44.707
But I'll never forget a patient of mine who said,

1525
01:03:44.707 --> 01:03:46.200
"You know, Dr. Lembke,

1526
01:03:46.200 --> 01:03:48.400
I realized I was an alcoholic

1527
01:03:48.400 --> 01:03:50.640
when I got started on an antidepressant,

1528
01:03:50.640 --> 01:03:53.040
wasn't depressed anymore, but kept drinking.

1529
01:03:53.040 --> 01:03:55.347
That was his aha moment because he was like,

1530
01:03:55.347 --> 01:03:57.730
"Oh, I thought I was drinking 'cause I was depressed.

1531
01:03:57.730 --> 01:04:00.300
But when I stopped being depressed, I was still drinking."

1532
01:04:00.300 --> 01:04:01.640
Yeah, that's kind of the genius

1533
01:04:01.640 --> 01:04:04.370
behind your dual diagnosis clinic, right?

1534
01:04:04.370 --> 01:04:08.100
It's almost a Trojan horse way of just treating addiction

1535
01:04:08.100 --> 01:04:10.870
because the way you get them in the door

1536
01:04:10.870 --> 01:04:13.190
in a non-threatening way is under the rubric

1537
01:04:13.190 --> 01:04:16.400
of treating their anxiety or depression, et cetera.

1538
01:04:16.400 --> 01:04:17.440
Right, yes.

1539
01:04:17.440 --> 01:04:19.820
Although it's not without effort

1540
01:04:19.820 --> 01:04:22.303
because people are resistant to that idea.

1541
01:04:23.530 --> 01:04:26.530
They come in and they want help with depression, anxiety.

1542
01:04:26.530 --> 01:04:29.560
They see the addiction as a secondary problem.

1543
01:04:29.560 --> 01:04:33.850
And it takes all my powers of persuasion

1544
01:04:33.850 --> 01:04:35.306
to get them onboard.

1545
01:04:35.306 --> 01:04:36.330
(both laughing)

1546
01:04:36.330 --> 01:04:38.230
Yeah, none too happy to be told

1547
01:04:38.230 --> 01:04:41.320
that you can't deal with your conditioned acts

1548
01:04:41.320 --> 01:04:42.980
until we deal with this substance

1549
01:04:42.980 --> 01:04:44.937
or behavioral addiction that you have

1550
01:04:44.937 --> 01:04:46.030
and they storm out,

1551
01:04:46.030 --> 01:04:49.030
and leave a one-star Yelp review or something.

1552
01:04:49.030 --> 01:04:51.510
Exactly. You've got it exactly.

1553
01:04:51.510 --> 01:04:52.544
You understand.

1554
01:04:52.544 --> 01:04:53.790
(both laughing)

1555
01:04:53.790 --> 01:04:55.510
Yeah, people don't wanna hear that you know.

1556
01:04:55.510 --> 01:04:56.630
It's interesting.

1557
01:04:56.630 --> 01:04:59.070
That piece, I mean, as a psychiatrist though,

1558
01:04:59.070 --> 01:05:02.740
you must have honed tools for how to communicate

1559
01:05:02.740 --> 01:05:05.070
with people to kinda crack that core.

1560
01:05:05.070 --> 01:05:06.257
Yes.

1561
01:05:06.257 --> 01:05:10.940
And the key piece is to be empathic

1562
01:05:10.940 --> 01:05:12.570
at the same time that you're telling people

1563
01:05:12.570 --> 01:05:13.920
what they don't wanna hear.

1564
01:05:14.790 --> 01:05:15.623
You know what I mean?
That's tough.

1565
01:05:15.623 --> 01:05:16.730
That's a tightrope walk.

1566
01:05:16.730 --> 01:05:17.790
It is, it is,

1567
01:05:17.790 --> 01:05:19.440
but I feel like I've gotten pretty good at it.

1568
01:05:19.440 --> 01:05:22.140
Having teenagers also helps

1569
01:05:22.140 --> 01:05:24.720
to sort of, you breathe and you stay calm

1570
01:05:24.720 --> 01:05:27.840
and you say, "I understand what you're saying."

1571
01:05:27.840 --> 01:05:30.540
So the typical sort of validating first.

1572
01:05:30.540 --> 01:05:34.020
But then you give them kind of the real deal,

1573
01:05:34.020 --> 01:05:35.940
you know, what the science shows,

1574
01:05:35.940 --> 01:05:38.420
what my clinical experience shows,

1575
01:05:38.420 --> 01:05:41.600
what I'm asking them to experiment with

1576
01:05:41.600 --> 01:05:43.070
so that they can gather their own data

1577
01:05:43.070 --> 01:05:45.150
and see if what I'm saying is right.

1578
01:05:45.150 --> 01:05:48.910
But on the data piece, I mean, this doesn't operate

1579
01:05:48.910 --> 01:05:50.290
in a logical framework.

1580
01:05:50.290 --> 01:05:51.740
It's an emotional framework.

1581
01:05:51.740 --> 01:05:53.650
And timing is so important, right?

1582
01:05:53.650 --> 01:05:57.120
Like I'm happy to talk sobriety with anyone who's suffering,

1583
01:05:57.120 --> 01:06:00.890
but I've learned to detach from any expectations

1584
01:06:00.890 --> 01:06:02.350
of what they will or won't do.

1585
01:06:02.350 --> 01:06:04.980
Like people get sober when they're ready to.

1586
01:06:04.980 --> 01:06:05.813
Yeah.

1587
01:06:05.813 --> 01:06:09.080
And one of the main things I have to teach my trainees,

1588
01:06:09.080 --> 01:06:13.500
my fellows, is that an essential part

1589
01:06:13.500 --> 01:06:14.710
of the work that we do

1590
01:06:14.710 --> 01:06:18.510
is that we have to actually really care about our patients,

1591
01:06:18.510 --> 01:06:21.440
but there is a point at which you can care too much.

1592
01:06:21.440 --> 01:06:24.260
And when we're trying harder than the patient is,

1593
01:06:24.260 --> 01:06:26.250
or wanting it more than the patient wants it,

1594
01:06:26.250 --> 01:06:27.850
we're not actually helping them.

1595
01:06:27.850 --> 01:06:29.700
Then you gotta go to the Al-Anon meeting.

1596
01:06:29.700 --> 01:06:30.620
That's right. That was good.
(Rich laughing)

1597
01:06:30.620 --> 01:06:33.590
Or we have to conduct our own little Al-Anon session,

1598
01:06:33.590 --> 01:06:35.100
you know, right there at work.

1599
01:06:35.100 --> 01:06:37.040
'Cause I work with a bunch of young folks,

1600
01:06:37.040 --> 01:06:38.690
which I can say is one of the most exciting things

1601
01:06:38.690 --> 01:06:40.660
that's happened in the last 10 years.

1602
01:06:40.660 --> 01:06:43.640
You know, 10 years ago, I couldn't find a medical student

1603
01:06:43.640 --> 01:06:45.450
under a rock who wanted to learn about addiction.

1604
01:06:45.450 --> 01:06:46.860
Now they're beating down my door,

1605
01:06:46.860 --> 01:06:48.070
which is really awesome.

1606
01:06:48.070 --> 01:06:49.810
Yeah, I think it brings up

1607
01:06:49.810 --> 01:06:53.110
an important kind of broader point

1608
01:06:53.110 --> 01:06:56.810
about culture in general

1609
01:06:56.810 --> 01:07:00.820
and this idea that we live in a situation

1610
01:07:00.820 --> 01:07:04.120
in which we are,

1611
01:07:04.120 --> 01:07:05.970
there's this asymmetry

1612
01:07:05.970 --> 01:07:07.790
in terms of how we approach our lives

1613
01:07:07.790 --> 01:07:09.420
with respect to pleasure and pain.

1614
01:07:09.420 --> 01:07:13.290
And we organize our lives completely around the pursuit

1615
01:07:13.290 --> 01:07:15.920
of pleasure and the avoidance of pain.

1616
01:07:15.920 --> 01:07:18.630
But as you kind of astutely put in the book,

1617
01:07:18.630 --> 01:07:20.660
the more that we pursue that,

1618
01:07:20.660 --> 01:07:24.050
ultimately the more pain that we reap.

1619
01:07:24.050 --> 01:07:27.880
Because these systems require a level of homeostasis

1620
01:07:27.880 --> 01:07:29.610
that we're constantly trying to avoid

1621
01:07:29.610 --> 01:07:34.470
so we're just delaying the inevitable onslaught of pain

1622
01:07:34.470 --> 01:07:37.470
that we so fear.

1623
01:07:37.470 --> 01:07:38.860
Yes, absolutely.

1624
01:07:38.860 --> 01:07:43.180
We live in a world in which we are saturated with dopamine

1625
01:07:43.180 --> 01:07:46.730
and we live in a culture which encourages us to pursue it.

1626
01:07:46.730 --> 01:07:50.100
But the ultimate end result of pursuing dopamine

1627
01:07:50.100 --> 01:07:52.760
is to feel worse than when you started.

1628
01:07:52.760 --> 01:07:55.113
And this is really the central message.

1629
01:07:56.560 --> 01:08:00.080
People are more depressed, more anxious,

1630
01:08:00.080 --> 01:08:01.880
more suicidal, and more addicted

1631
01:08:01.880 --> 01:08:03.600
than they were 30 years ago.

1632
01:08:03.600 --> 01:08:06.500
And I contend that one of the main reasons

1633
01:08:06.500 --> 01:08:10.690
is because of this relentless pursuit of pleasure

1634
01:08:10.690 --> 01:08:14.340
that essentially adjusts the dopamine levels,

1635
01:08:14.340 --> 01:08:18.270
changes the hedonic or pleasure set point

1636
01:08:18.270 --> 01:08:21.733
to make people anhedonic meaning without joy.

1637
01:08:22.810 --> 01:08:24.890
The godfather of integrative medicine

1638
01:08:24.890 --> 01:08:27.960
and a true pioneer of health, Dr. Andrew Weil

1639
01:08:27.960 --> 01:08:32.010
is a legend in the realm of mind/body healing.

1640
01:08:32.010 --> 01:08:35.490
In the 606 drop, we talked psychedelics,

1641
01:08:35.490 --> 01:08:38.430
fungi, food, and breath,

1642
01:08:38.430 --> 01:08:41.030
and the best practices you can adopt

1643
01:08:41.030 --> 01:08:44.740
to sidestep chronic lifestyle disease.

1644
01:08:44.740 --> 01:08:46.640
What goes on in your mind

1645
01:08:46.640 --> 01:08:49.640
really determines and shapes your experience

1646
01:08:49.640 --> 01:08:50.883
of the external world.

1647
01:08:51.840 --> 01:08:54.430
And I've applied that very much in my work with patients

1648
01:08:54.430 --> 01:08:57.770
because I just find over and over again

1649
01:08:57.770 --> 01:09:00.380
that the root causes of illness are often

1650
01:09:00.380 --> 01:09:03.380
in the mental-emotional sphere and not in the physical body,

1651
01:09:03.380 --> 01:09:05.240
or like they're in both.

1652
01:09:05.240 --> 01:09:07.730
But conventional medicine has so ignored

1653
01:09:07.730 --> 01:09:10.447
that non-physical part of ourselves

1654
01:09:10.447 --> 01:09:12.270
and I think that's a great limitation.

1655
01:09:12.270 --> 01:09:14.750
Yeah, I mean, I think one of the things

1656
01:09:14.750 --> 01:09:17.600
I appreciate about you the most is just

1657
01:09:18.600 --> 01:09:22.980
that level of cognizance and emphasis

1658
01:09:22.980 --> 01:09:26.800
that you put on the mind and your willingness

1659
01:09:26.800 --> 01:09:30.200
to delve deep into the nature of consciousness.

1660
01:09:30.200 --> 01:09:31.710
And these are just anathema

1661
01:09:31.710 --> 01:09:34.470
in conventional medical practice.

1662
01:09:34.470 --> 01:09:35.760
Yeah. It just doesn't compute.

1663
01:09:35.760 --> 01:09:38.140
I took, one of the most interesting courses

1664
01:09:38.140 --> 01:09:40.880
I ever took was in medical hypnosis

1665
01:09:40.880 --> 01:09:43.273
at Columbia University, it was for physicians.

1666
01:09:44.110 --> 01:09:47.590
I learned so many fascinating things there.

1667
01:09:47.590 --> 01:09:51.270
And as a result of that, I made mind-body medicine,

1668
01:09:51.270 --> 01:09:54.550
a major component of the integrative medicine curriculum

1669
01:09:54.550 --> 01:09:55.383
that I developed.

1670
01:09:55.383 --> 01:09:57.840
And I very frequently refer patients

1671
01:09:57.840 --> 01:09:59.510
to mind-body practitioners,

1672
01:09:59.510 --> 01:10:02.000
whether that's hypnotherapists

1673
01:10:02.000 --> 01:10:04.140
or visualization practitioners

1674
01:10:04.140 --> 01:10:06.790
or any one of a number of other modalities,

1675
01:10:06.790 --> 01:10:09.880
because I find these things to be incredibly effective,

1676
01:10:09.880 --> 01:10:12.430
cost-effective, I mean, even fun

1677
01:10:12.430 --> 01:10:14.420
for both practitioner and patient.

1678
01:10:14.420 --> 01:10:17.150
And relatively free of risk.

1679
01:10:17.150 --> 01:10:20.540
Totally. And they are so underutilized in medicine.

1680
01:10:20.540 --> 01:10:25.020
And I remember once I was challenged to a public debate

1681
01:10:25.020 --> 01:10:26.010
by Arnold Relman,

1682
01:10:26.010 --> 01:10:29.140
who was the editor of "The New England Journal of Medicine".

1683
01:10:29.140 --> 01:10:31.560
And it was a very publicized event.

1684
01:10:31.560 --> 01:10:35.395
And I mean, I have never met a more closed-minded person.

1685
01:10:35.395 --> 01:10:38.950
His position was all you have to do is show me the evidence.

1686
01:10:38.950 --> 01:10:40.990
You know, you show me the evidence, blah, blah, blah.

1687
01:10:40.990 --> 01:10:42.697
So anything you'd show him, he'd say,

1688
01:10:42.697 --> 01:10:44.467
"Oh, well, that's not evidence."

1689
01:10:44.467 --> 01:10:46.350
And one of the questions that I asked him,

1690
01:10:46.350 --> 01:10:49.530
I said that the best researched area of what I do

1691
01:10:49.530 --> 01:10:51.070
is mind-body interaction.

1692
01:10:51.070 --> 01:10:54.450
There's been 30 years of studies on that.

1693
01:10:54.450 --> 01:10:57.630
And yet these modalities are so underutilized.

1694
01:10:57.630 --> 01:10:59.780
What would you do to increase their use?

1695
01:10:59.780 --> 01:11:01.460
And his response was, "There is no evidence

1696
01:11:01.460 --> 01:11:03.320
for mind-body interactions."

1697
01:11:03.320 --> 01:11:05.320
So, you know, (chuckles) I don't know

1698
01:11:05.320 --> 01:11:06.153
what you can do with that.

1699
01:11:06.153 --> 01:11:08.240
Yeah, I mean, how are you...

1700
01:11:08.240 --> 01:11:11.550
Yeah, there's a profound hubris

1701
01:11:11.550 --> 01:11:14.240
around the scientific method.

1702
01:11:14.240 --> 01:11:16.660
Science is truth. Science is fact.

1703
01:11:16.660 --> 01:11:21.140
But that overlooks the fact that it's not,

1704
01:11:21.140 --> 01:11:22.200
it's a method.

1705
01:11:22.200 --> 01:11:23.033
Yeah.

1706
01:11:23.033 --> 01:11:24.449
It's not a fact in and of itself.

1707
01:11:24.449 --> 01:11:27.410
It's a means of approximating truth.

1708
01:11:27.410 --> 01:11:29.270
It's a way to approach or discover truth

1709
01:11:29.270 --> 01:11:32.880
but it's also not the sole method and it's flawed.

1710
01:11:32.880 --> 01:11:34.500
And I think there's so much arrogance

1711
01:11:34.500 --> 01:11:36.740
around this is the way that we do it.

1712
01:11:36.740 --> 01:11:39.680
And that kind of approach

1713
01:11:39.680 --> 01:11:43.440
is very dualistic or binary

1714
01:11:43.440 --> 01:11:45.820
in that it's controlling for this variable

1715
01:11:45.820 --> 01:11:47.130
or this and that.

1716
01:11:47.130 --> 01:11:49.830
And obviously we've made, I'm not dismissing it.

1717
01:11:49.830 --> 01:11:52.000
It's like, this is-
No, of course.

1718
01:11:52.000 --> 01:11:54.090
the engine of progress, of course.

1719
01:11:54.090 --> 01:11:57.297
But it overlooks the holistic nature of the human body

1720
01:11:57.297 --> 01:11:58.320
and the interplay

1721
01:11:58.320 --> 01:12:00.900
between all of these very sensitive systems.

1722
01:12:00.900 --> 01:12:03.770
And science and medicine in our part of the world

1723
01:12:03.770 --> 01:12:07.030
are totally, totally dominated by materialism.

1724
01:12:07.030 --> 01:12:08.540
You know, a philosophy that says

1725
01:12:08.540 --> 01:12:11.820
that the only thing that's real is that which can be seen

1726
01:12:11.820 --> 01:12:14.090
and touched and measured.

1727
01:12:14.090 --> 01:12:17.470
And that if you see a change in a physical system,

1728
01:12:17.470 --> 01:12:19.320
the cause has to be physical.

1729
01:12:19.320 --> 01:12:23.250
Non-physical causation of physical events doesn't compute.

1730
01:12:23.250 --> 01:12:24.570
It's not allowed for.

1731
01:12:24.570 --> 01:12:28.380
So, in hypnosis there are so many demonstrations,

1732
01:12:28.380 --> 01:12:32.000
like you can touch a person with a finger

1733
01:12:32.000 --> 01:12:35.649
and you have them believe it's a piece of red, hot metal

1734
01:12:35.649 --> 01:12:36.670
and if they're in a deep trance,

1735
01:12:36.670 --> 01:12:38.270
they get an actual blister.

1736
01:12:38.270 --> 01:12:41.130
I mean, that is non-physical causation of a physical event.

1737
01:12:41.130 --> 01:12:45.850
So if you try to get a doctor to look at that or scientists,

1738
01:12:45.850 --> 01:12:47.130
oh, it's just a curious thing.

1739
01:12:47.130 --> 01:12:48.633
It doesn't have any meaning.

1740
01:12:49.760 --> 01:12:52.980
Yeah, that kind of makes me think

1741
01:12:52.980 --> 01:12:55.820
of your interesting ideas around the placebo effect

1742
01:12:55.820 --> 01:12:57.180
and the way that we kind of think

1743
01:12:57.180 --> 01:12:58.610
about that backwards.

1744
01:12:58.610 --> 01:13:01.440
Fortunately, that's one of the things that's changing now,

1745
01:13:01.440 --> 01:13:03.260
which makes me very happy.

1746
01:13:03.260 --> 01:13:05.460
But still the most common ways

1747
01:13:05.460 --> 01:13:08.330
I hear the word placebo used are in phrases

1748
01:13:08.330 --> 01:13:11.640
like how do you know that's not just a placebo effect

1749
01:13:11.640 --> 01:13:14.030
and the most interesting word there is just.

1750
01:13:14.030 --> 01:13:17.850
Or we have to rule out the placebo effect.

1751
01:13:17.850 --> 01:13:19.500
That's what we wanna rule in.

1752
01:13:19.500 --> 01:13:22.600
The placebo response is pure healing from within

1753
01:13:22.600 --> 01:13:23.920
mediated by the mind,

1754
01:13:23.920 --> 01:13:26.150
unmixed up with the direct effects of treatment,

1755
01:13:26.150 --> 01:13:27.560
which are likely to be harmful.

1756
01:13:27.560 --> 01:13:28.880
Yeah, I think I've heard you talk

1757
01:13:28.880 --> 01:13:32.600
about the fact that in every kind

1758
01:13:32.600 --> 01:13:34.530
of double blind trial-

1759
01:13:34.530 --> 01:13:35.860
Yes, exactly.
if you look

1760
01:13:35.860 --> 01:13:39.770
at the placebo group, there's always a few people

1761
01:13:39.770 --> 01:13:41.960
that have experienced the result

1762
01:13:41.960 --> 01:13:43.218
as if they had taken-
All of the changes.

1763
01:13:43.218 --> 01:13:44.473
Exactly. All of it.

1764
01:13:44.473 --> 01:13:46.514
And that's the most interesting thing

1765
01:13:46.514 --> 01:13:48.240
of the whole thing.
Amazing that any change

1766
01:13:48.240 --> 01:13:50.670
we can produce with a pharmacological agent

1767
01:13:50.670 --> 01:13:52.180
can be exactly mimicked

1768
01:13:52.180 --> 01:13:53.240
in at least some people,

1769
01:13:53.240 --> 01:13:56.420
some of the time by a mind mediated mechanism.

1770
01:13:56.420 --> 01:13:58.250
So fortunately the change that's happened,

1771
01:13:58.250 --> 01:13:59.320
and this is the result

1772
01:13:59.320 --> 01:14:01.930
of these new brain imaging technologies,

1773
01:14:01.930 --> 01:14:05.460
is that now you can show that certain parts

1774
01:14:05.460 --> 01:14:06.450
of the brain are active

1775
01:14:06.450 --> 01:14:08.130
when people have placebo responses

1776
01:14:08.130 --> 01:14:12.170
and this makes it accessible and real to people.

1777
01:14:12.170 --> 01:14:14.880
So I think placebos are being taken much more seriously.

1778
01:14:14.880 --> 01:14:17.940
Right, because now the imaging science

1779
01:14:17.940 --> 01:14:20.560
has caught up and neuroscience has progressed to the point

1780
01:14:20.560 --> 01:14:24.290
where they can provide an explanation that makes sense

1781
01:14:24.290 --> 01:14:25.603
to the conventional

1782
01:14:25.603 --> 01:14:26.436
kind of community.
Exactly.

1783
01:14:26.436 --> 01:14:28.540
And the same thing's happened with meditation.

1784
01:14:28.540 --> 01:14:30.650
There've been a tremendous number of studies

1785
01:14:30.650 --> 01:14:33.340
of very longtime serious meditators

1786
01:14:33.340 --> 01:14:36.570
showing different activity in different brain areas

1787
01:14:36.570 --> 01:14:37.403
as a result.

1788
01:14:37.403 --> 01:14:40.120
So the fact that a meditation practice

1789
01:14:40.120 --> 01:14:42.480
can actually physically change the brain,

1790
01:14:42.480 --> 01:14:44.126
I mean, that's really interesting stuff.

1791
01:14:44.126 --> 01:14:45.860
Yeah, yeah. Yeah.

1792
01:14:45.860 --> 01:14:47.390
But within that,

1793
01:14:47.390 --> 01:14:50.240
there's this profound, deep appreciation

1794
01:14:50.240 --> 01:14:51.990
for the mystical, right?
Yes. Yeah.

1795
01:14:51.990 --> 01:14:54.050
Which is like my favorite thing to talk about.

1796
01:14:54.050 --> 01:14:55.500
Oh good. (laughing)

1797
01:14:55.500 --> 01:14:58.670
And I suspect that was in part informed

1798
01:14:58.670 --> 01:15:02.890
by all these shamans and various healing practitioners

1799
01:15:02.890 --> 01:15:04.740
that you've experienced over the decades

1800
01:15:04.740 --> 01:15:07.630
in South America and Asia, et cetera.

1801
01:15:07.630 --> 01:15:11.700
One of the attitudes that I just can't stand

1802
01:15:11.700 --> 01:15:14.950
is that when scientists believe that it's their job

1803
01:15:14.950 --> 01:15:16.910
to make mystery go away.

1804
01:15:16.910 --> 01:15:19.750
You know, it seems to me, mystery is an essential aspect

1805
01:15:19.750 --> 01:15:21.710
of the universe,

1806
01:15:21.710 --> 01:15:24.833
and that you have to appreciate that and wonder at it.

1807
01:15:26.040 --> 01:15:28.420
Once, Terence McKenna, I once heard say

1808
01:15:28.420 --> 01:15:30.890
that the bigger you build the fire,

1809
01:15:30.890 --> 01:15:33.530
the more you're aware of the extent of the darkness.

1810
01:15:33.530 --> 01:15:35.450
And I think that's true.

1811
01:15:35.450 --> 01:15:38.370
And I think

1812
01:15:38.370 --> 01:15:40.830
that our experience,

1813
01:15:40.830 --> 01:15:42.560
at the heart of our experience is mystery,

1814
01:15:42.560 --> 01:15:44.850
the stuff that we can appreciate

1815
01:15:44.850 --> 01:15:47.050
but we can't really understand with our mind.

1816
01:15:47.050 --> 01:15:48.090
Right.

1817
01:15:48.090 --> 01:15:51.120
What does it mean to be human?

1818
01:15:51.120 --> 01:15:53.970
How do you be a good person?

1819
01:15:53.970 --> 01:15:55.950
What happens when you die?

1820
01:15:55.950 --> 01:15:59.470
If you find yourself grappling with these big questions,

1821
01:15:59.470 --> 01:16:02.140
then my audio experiment with Rainn Wilson

1822
01:16:02.140 --> 01:16:06.030
and Reza Aslan, hosts of "Metaphysical Milkshake"

1823
01:16:06.030 --> 01:16:10.040
back on episode 633 is definitely your jam.

